1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Dark, subterranean subway station in a dystopian metropolis. Massive swarms of anthropomorphic rodents in business attire push and shove frantically through turnstiles beneath overhead directional signage pointing vaguely toward "Nowhere".

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:25.000
The protagonist rat ascends into a grim, rain-slicked urban center. Giant commercial billboards dominate the skyline, substituting major corporate logos (Nike, McDonald’s, Apple, Starbucks, Visa) with the singular neon mandate: "Happiness".

3
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Corporate marketing conditions the crowds to equate material acquisition with inner fulfillment. Storefront window displays flash manufactured promises that true self-worth can be bought, carried in shopping bags, or worn.

4
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:52.000
A ferocious consumer frenzy erupts. Neon clearance banners flash urgency, and crowds of manic rodents stampede store doors in a violent Black Friday retail riot.

5
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Rats brutally brawl and trample one another over boxed electronics and cheap consumer goods. The protagonist grabs multiple shopping bags, but is violently tackled, beaten down, and stripped of all purchases, left stranded and empty-handed on the pavement.

6
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:18.000
The morning commute resumes. Rats cram shoulder-to-shoulder inside suffocating subway cars, expressionless and alienated, staring blankly ahead in collective emotional isolation.

7
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Seeking individual autonomy, the protagonist purchases a sleek red sports car. The promised freedom is instantly extinguished as he becomes trapped in an endless, catastrophic multilane highway gridlock with thousands of identical frustrated drivers.

8
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Digital highway billboards continue flashing luxury status symbols, fast food, and perfumes, perpetually reinforcing the cognitive illusion that the next purchase will finally relieve existential boredom.

9
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Exhausted and hollowed out by failed consumerism, the protagonist turns to chemical escapism through alcohol. Neon signs parody liquor brands: "Drink, forget, smile", Jack Daniel’s-style "Drink the blues away", and "Absolute Happiness".

10
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:12.000
The protagonist downs bottles of hard liquor in a rain-drenched alleyway, desperately seeking sensory numbness to quiet urban hyper-stimulation and internal despair.

11
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Severely intoxicated, the rat collapses face-down on the filthy sidewalk. Crowds of hurrying commuters step callously over his unconscious body without a second glance or shred of empathy.

12
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Dawn breaks over the cold cityscape. The protagonist awakens shivering, hungover, and physically degraded on the dirty curb, facing raw existential dread and financial depletion.

13
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:52.000
His eyes lock onto an enormous pharmaceutical billboard advertising prescription pills labeled "Happiness" (Fluoxetine 200mg—a massive, chemically absurd dosage marketed by corporate chemistry).

14
00:02:52.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Desperate for psychological relief, the rat obtains and swallows the bright red pill. Instant neurochemical modulation begins to flood his nervous system.

15
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:20.000
The visual aesthetic dramatically transforms from bleak industrial grime into vibrant, pastel 1930s rubber-hose animation set to Edvard Grieg’s "Morning Mood". The rat floats weightlessly through whimsical skies, perceiving the dystopian city as a joyful fairytale playground.

16
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Chemically induced euphoria peaks as the protagonist tap-dances across rainbow clouds and smiles blissfully, entirely decoupled from the material misery of his physical reality.

17
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:45.000
The pharmaceutical high abruptly wears off. The vibrant colors violently drain away, slamming the rat back into the cold, wet, noisy urban reality with amplified psychological sensory shock.

18
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Suffering acute withdrawal and panic, the rat frantically checks his empty wallet to buy another pill, realizing he is completely broke and out of funds.

19
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Spotting a crisp green dollar bill lying ahead on the floor, the desperate rodent lunges forward, failing to notice that the currency is resting directly on the trigger plate of a giant spring-loaded mousetrap.

20
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:16.000
The heavy steel spring snaps violently shut onto his neck. The camera pulls backward to reveal an endless corporate office tower where millions of identical rats are permanently trapped in cubicle mousetraps, endlessly slaving as wage cogs in the machinery. End credits roll to Georges Bizet’s "Carmen: Habanera".
